// ALG1 Unit 05 Lesson 01 Quiz
// Lesson: Polynomial Operations and Radical Readiness
// MLA Standards: MLA.A1.FND.3; MLA.A1.FND.6; MLA.A1.FND.7

::ALG1_U05_L01_Q01::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L01_Q01] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.6]
Simplify \((2x^2+3x)+(4x^2-x)\).
{
=A. 6x^2+2x#Correct. Combine \(2x^2+4x^2\) and \(3x-x\).
~B. 6x^4+2x#Adding like terms does not add exponents.
~C. 2x^2+2x#This leaves out the \(4x^2\) term.
~D. 6x^2+4x#This adds the x-coefficients incorrectly.




}

::ALG1_U05_L01_Q02::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L01_Q02] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.3]
What is the square root of 64?
{
~A. 6#\(6^2=36\), not 64.
~B. 32#This divides 64 by 2 instead of finding the square root.
=C. 8#Correct. \(8^2=64\).
~D. -8#The principal square root is nonnegative.




}

::ALG1_U05_L01_Q03::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L01_Q03] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.6]
Simplify \((7x^2-2x+1)-(3x^2+x-4)\).
{
~A. 4x^2-x-3#This does not distribute the subtraction to every term.
=B. 4x^2-3x+5#Correct. \(7x^2-2x+1-3x^2-x+4=4x^2-3x+5\).
~C. 10x^2-x-3#This adds the \(x^2\) terms instead of subtracting the second polynomial.
~D. 4x^2+3x+5#The x-term should be \(-2x-x=-3x\).




}

::ALG1_U05_L01_Q04::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L01_Q04] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.6]
Simplify \(3x(2x-5)\).
{
~A. 6x-15#The first term should include \(x^2\) because \(x\cdot x=x^2\).
~B. 5x^2-15x#This adds coefficients 3 and 2 instead of multiplying them.
~C. 6x^2-5x#The \(-5\) must also be multiplied by \(3x\).
=D. 6x^2-15x#Correct. Distribute \(3x\) to both terms.




}

::ALG1_U05_L01_Q05::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L01_Q05] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.7]
Simplify \((15x^4-10x^2)/(5x^2)\).
{
=A. 3x^2-2#Correct. Divide each term by \(5x^2\).
~B. 3x^2-2x#The second term \(10x^2/(5x^2)\) simplifies to 2, not \(2x\).
~C. 10x^2-5#This subtracts 5 from each coefficient instead of dividing.
~D. 3x^4-2x^2#This divides coefficients but does not subtract exponents.




}

::ALG1_U05_L01_Q06::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L01_Q06] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.3]
Which number is a perfect square?
{
~A. 50#50 is between \(7^2=49\) and \(8^2=64\).
=B. 49#Correct. \(49=7^2\), so it is a perfect square.
~C. 72#72 is not the square of an integer.
~D. 90#90 is not the square of an integer.




}

::ALG1_U05_L01_Q07::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L01_Q07] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.6]
Simplify \((x+4)(x+2)\).
{
~A. x^2+8#This multiplies only first and last terms and misses the middle terms.
~B. x^2+6#The expression must include the x-terms from distribution.
~C. 2x^2+6x+8#The first term is \(x\cdot x=x^2\), not \(2x^2\).
=D. x^2+6x+8#Correct. \(x^2+2x+4x+8=x^2+6x+8\).




}

::ALG1_U05_L01_Q08::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L01_Q08] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.6]
Simplify \((4x^2-3)+(x^2+9)\).
{
~A. 4x^2+6#This leaves out the \(x^2\) from the second polynomial.
~B. 5x^4+6#Adding \(x^2\) terms does not make \(x^4\).
~C. 3x^2+6#This subtracts the \(x^2\) coefficients instead of adding them.
=D. 5x^2+6#Correct. \(4x^2+x^2=5x^2\) and \(-3+9=6\).




}

::ALG1_U05_L01_Q09::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L01_Q09] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.7]
Simplify \((18x^3+6x)/(6x)\).
{
=A. 3x^2+1#Correct. \(18x^3/(6x)=3x^2\) and \(6x/(6x)=1\).
~B. 3x^2+x#The second term simplifies to 1, not x.
~C. 12x^2#This subtracts coefficients and combines unlike terms.
~D. 3x^3+1#The first term should subtract exponents, leaving \(x^2\).




}

::ALG1_U05_L01_Q10::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L01_Q10] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.3]
What is the square root of 121?
{
~A. 10#\(10^2=100\), not 121.
~B. 12#\(12^2=144\), not 121.
~C. 60.5#This divides 121 by 2 instead of finding its square root.
=D. 11#Correct. \(11^2=121\).




}

::ALG1_U05_L01_Q11::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L01_Q11] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.6]
Simplify \(5x^2-2x^2+7x\).
{
~A. 3x^4+7x#Subtracting like terms does not add exponents.
=B. 3x^2+7x#Correct. \(5x^2-2x^2=3x^2\), and \(7x\) remains.
~C. 7x^2+7x#This adds 5 and 2 instead of subtracting.
~D. 3x^2+7#The \(7x\) term is not a constant.




}

::ALG1_U05_L01_Q12::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L01_Q12] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.6]
Simplify \(-2x(4x-3)\).
{
~A. -8x+6#The first term should include \(x^2\).
~B. -8x^2-6x#The second product is positive because a negative times a negative is positive.
=C. -8x^2+6x#Correct. \(-2x\cdot4x=-8x^2\) and \(-2x\cdot-3=6x\).
~D. 8x^2+6x#The first product should be negative.




}

::ALG1_U05_L01_Q13::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L01_Q13] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.7]
Simplify \((8x^2+12x)/(4x)\).
{
=A. 2x+3#Correct. Divide each term by \(4x\).
~B. 2x+3x#The second term \(12x/(4x)\) simplifies to 3.
~C. 4x+8#This subtracts instead of divides.
~D. 2x^2+3x#This divides coefficients but not variable powers.




}

::ALG1_U05_L01_Q14::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L01_Q14] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.3]
Which expression equals 6?
{
~A. the square root of 12#The square root of 12 is not 6 because \(6^2=36\).
~B. the square root of 30#The square root of 30 is not 6.
=C. the square root of 36#Correct. \(6^2=36\).
~D. the square root of 42#The square root of 42 is greater than 6 but not exactly 6.




}

::ALG1_U05_L01_Q15::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L01_Q15] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.6]
Simplify \((x-5)(x+5)\).
{
~A. x^2+25#The middle terms cancel, and \(-5\cdot5=-25\).
~B. x^2+10x-25#The \(+5x\) and \(-5x\) terms cancel.
~C. 2x-25#The product of x and x is \(x^2\), not \(2x\).
=D. x^2-25#Correct. This is a difference of squares pattern.




}

::ALG1_U05_L01_Q16::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L01_Q16] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.6]
Simplify \((6x^2+x-8)+(x^2-4x+3)\).
{
~A. 7x^2+5x-5#The x-terms should be \(x-4x=-3x\).
=B. 7x^2-3x-5#Correct. Combine matching powers and constants.
~C. 5x^2-3x-5#The \(x^2\) terms should be added, not subtracted.
~D. 7x^4-3x-5#Adding \(x^2\) terms does not create \(x^4\).




}

::ALG1_U05_L01_Q17::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L01_Q17] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.7]
Simplify \((9x^5)/(3x^2)\).
{
=A. 3x^3#Correct. Divide coefficients and subtract exponents: 5 minus 2 gives \(x^3\).
~B. 3x^7#Exponents are subtracted when dividing powers with the same base.
~C. 6x^3#Coefficients should be divided, not subtracted.
~D. 3x^2#The exponent should be \(5-2=3\).




}

::ALG1_U05_L01_Q18::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L01_Q18] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.6]
Simplify \(4x^2+3x-9x^2+2x\).
{
~A. -5x^2+5#The \(x\)-terms combine to \(5x\), not 5.
=B. -5x^2+5x#Correct. \(4x^2-9x^2=-5x^2\) and \(3x+2x=5x\).
~C. 13x^2+5x#This adds \(4x^2\) and \(9x^2\) instead of subtracting.
~D. -5x^4+5x#Combining \(x^2\) terms does not add exponents.




}

::ALG1_U05_L01_Q19::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L01_Q19] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.3]
Which value is between the square root of 25 and the square root of 49?
{
~A. 4#\(the square root of 25=5\), so 4 is less than the lower endpoint.
~B. 5#This equals the square root of 25, not between the two values.
=C. 6#Correct. \(the square root of 25=5\) and \(the square root of 49=7\), so 6 is between them.
~D. 8#8 is greater than \(the square root of 49=7\).




}

::ALG1_U05_L01_Q20::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L01_Q20] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.6]
Simplify \((2x+3)(x-4)\).
{
~A. 2x^2-12#This misses the middle terms.
~B. 2x^2+5x-12#The middle terms should combine to \(-5x\), not \(+5x\).
=C. 2x^2-5x-12#Correct. \(2x^2-8x+3x-12=2x^2-5x-12\).
~D. 2x^2-8x+3#The \(3\) must also multiply by \(-4\).




}

::ALG1_U05_L01_Q21::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L01_Q21] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.7]
Simplify \((20x^4-5x^2)/(5x^2)\).
{
=A. 4x^2-1#Correct. Divide each term by \(5x^2\).
~B. 4x^2-x#The second term simplifies to 1.
~C. 15x^2#This subtracts coefficients and omits the second term.
~D. 4x^4-1x^2#This divides coefficients but does not subtract exponents.




}

::ALG1_U05_L01_Q22::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L01_Q22] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.6]
Simplify \((3x^2-7x+4)-(x^2-2x+9)\).
{
~A. 2x^2-9x+13#This does not distribute subtraction to the second polynomial correctly.
~B. 4x^2-9x+13#This adds the \(x^2\) terms and constants.
=C. 2x^2-5x-5#Correct. \(3x^2-7x+4-x^2+2x-9=2x^2-5x-5\).
~D. 2x^2-5x+13#The constant is \(4-9=-5\).




}

::ALG1_U05_L01_Q23::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L01_Q23] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.3]
What is the square root of 144?
{
~A. 10#\(10^2=100\), not 144.
=B. 12#Correct. \(12^2=144\).
~C. 11#\(11^2=121\), not 144.
~D. 72#This divides 144 by 2 instead of finding the square root.




}

::ALG1_U05_L01_Q24::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L01_Q24] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.6]
Simplify \((x+6)^2\).
{
~A. x^2+36#This misses the middle term from multiplying two binomials.
~B. x^2+6x+36#The middle term should be \(12x\).
~C. 2x+36#The product \(x\cdot x\) is \(x^2\).
=D. x^2+12x+36#Correct. \((x+6)(x+6)=x^2+12x+36\).




}

::ALG1_U05_L01_Q25::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L01_Q25] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.7]
Simplify \((14x^3+21x^2)/(7x)\).
{
=A. 2x^2+3x#Correct. Divide each term by \(7x\).
~B. 2x^3+3x^2#This divides coefficients but leaves exponents unchanged.
~C. 7x^2+14x#This subtracts coefficients instead of dividing.
~D. 2x^2+3#The second term leaves \(3x\), not 3.




}


